### 0008 — Streamlit's Rerun Model: How app/dashboard.py Actually Works

**Date added:** 2026-07-15
**Status:** Lesson generated on request ("this is genuinely amazing! ... /teach how to use the tool
and what it can do") immediately after a live, hands-on run of the dashboard (pipeline from cache,
Oil Volume offline demo, and the microstructure panel's known import bug, all observed directly).

**Context:** Two prior orientation lessons already exist in this repo's history — 0002
(`docs/learning-0002-and-microstructure-plan` branch, "system map & usage") and 0006
(`feature/vhdl-flat-book` branch, "Driving the HFT Lab") — both at the whole-repo, architecture
level. Neither goes deep on the Streamlit dashboard specifically. This lesson fills that gap: it
teaches Streamlit's core non-obvious mechanic (full-script rerun on every interaction, no partial
callbacks) and walks three real `session_state` guards in `app/dashboard.py` that exist *because*
of that mechanic — the pipeline-result cache, the sticky Oil-Volume-loaded flag, and the
ITCH-parse cache in the microstructure panel. It also turns the live-observed
"signals_cpp extension not built" warning into a grounded teaching moment (bare `import
signals_cpp` in `events.py` vs. the try/fallback pattern already used in `mm_analysis.py`).

**Files:** [lessons/0008-streamlit-dashboard-tour.html](lessons/0008-streamlit-dashboard-tour.html)
(drill/vocab not yet generated — `/drill-me streamlit-dashboard-tour` next).

**Key concepts covered:** Streamlit's full-script-rerun model vs. traditional event-callback GUIs
(Tkinter/PyQt); `st.session_state` as the only thing that survives a rerun; three concrete
session_state guards in this repo's own dashboard and why each exists; the bare-import vs.
try/fallback contrast for a compiled pybind11 extension across an editable install.

**Source files studied:** `app/dashboard.py` (in full), `src/oil_alpha/microstructure/events.py`,
`src/oil_alpha/microstructure/mm_analysis.py`.

**Note on numbering:** 0007 is reserved for the queued constant-cycle-hardware
teach-target (see Backlog below), and 0002 exists on the unmerged
`docs/learning-0002-and-microstructure-plan` branch, so this lesson uses 0008.
The PROGRESS/GLOSSARY divergence across the July-2026 PR stack (mm-sim, vhdl,
this lesson) was reconciled into this canonical union when the stack merged.

