### 0007 — Constant-Cycle Hardware vs Range-Linear Software (teach-target)

**Date added:** 2026-07-07
**Status:** Teach-target queued — lesson/drill/vocab not yet generated

**Context:** Queued after the `vhdl-flat-book` plan landed. The C++ flat book scans
`O(range)` levels to find the best bid/ask (the same O(N·R) cost lesson 0005 measured),
while `hw/vhdl/flat_book.vhd` resolves best-of-DEPTH in a **priority encoder** whose
latency is a fixed number of clock cycles regardless of DEPTH. The GHDL sweep proves it:
`test_cycles_per_op_constant_across_depth_sweep` asserts cycles==10000 at both DEPTH=1024
and DEPTH=4096 — range-independence a software scan can never claim.

**Files:** to be generated (`/teach constant-cycle hardware priority encoder`).

**Key concepts to cover:** functional simulation vs synthesis/timing (GHDL proves
behaviour, not gate delay); priority encoder as parallel best-of-N; process VARIABLE
vs SIGNAL (same-cycle visibility in the best-scan); registered outputs / one-op-per-clock;
file-driven testbench parity against a C++ oracle (`std.textio`, schema-pinned CSV);
why the same O(range) scan that is a cost in software is a constant in hardware.

**Source files to study:** `hw/vhdl/flat_book.vhd`, `hw/vhdl/flat_book_tb.vhd`,
`cpp/itch/golden_vectors_main.cpp`, `scripts/run_vhdl_sim.sh`,
